[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186199502 :tutorial/content "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote ![](images/6f/6fe1832d2f4ae2b36446386e5d0ec7c41f898f4e575af9238695c37f7c427250.svg) and ![](images/49/496eacb98651e3fab4c0d0a7b44ab283fadad1c07243da485aeb0c4a059d5712.svg) then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d6/d696700e295d9f43111ac53602b936a991b7766af654cedaa5afdae21b088a59.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n![](images/de/de2a75a6681e1e393b21b60de9d7aeee748a8e6d04010ab713b40f0f41f239cf.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x-a & y-b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}x & y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a & b\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)}}+{{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186199502 :tutorial/content "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote ![](images/6f/6fe1832d2f4ae2b36446386e5d0ec7c41f898f4e575af9238695c37f7c427250.svg) and ![](images/49/496eacb98651e3fab4c0d0a7b44ab283fadad1c07243da485aeb0c4a059d5712.svg) then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d6/d696700e295d9f43111ac53602b936a991b7766af654cedaa5afdae21b088a59.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n![](images/de/de2a75a6681e1e393b21b60de9d7aeee748a8e6d04010ab713b40f0f41f239cf.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x-a & y-b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}x & y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a & b\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)}}+{{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$"]] "retractions" [[17592186199502 :tutorial/content "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote ![](images/6f/6fe1832d2f4ae2b36446386e5d0ec7c41f898f4e575af9238695c37f7c427250.svg) and ![](images/49/496eacb98651e3fab4c0d0a7b44ab283fadad1c07243da485aeb0c4a059d5712.svg) then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d6/d696700e295d9f43111ac53602b936a991b7766af654cedaa5afdae21b088a59.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =f(a)+\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n![](images/de/de2a75a6681e1e393b21b60de9d7aeee748a8e6d04010ab713b40f0f41f239cf.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x-a & y-b\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}x & y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}a & b\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)}}+{{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$"]]}]